        "theme":  "Philosophy",
        "summary":  "David Linthicum argues that recent OpenAI security incidents reveal governance failures driven by human oversight deficiencies rather than inherent AI dangers. He contends that the misalignment reports reflect issues seen throughout computing history and warns against allowing science fiction narratives to drive enterprise AI strategy. The piece calls for proportionate, evidence-based risk assessment rather than AI-specific fear frameworks.",
        "execSummary":  "This opinion piece challenges the dominant enterprise narrative that AI systems are uniquely dangerous, positioning most AI incidents as human governance failures that would occur with any powerful technology. For enterprise leaders, the practical implication is that existing risk management frameworks—access controls, audit trails, change management—are largely the right tools for AI governance, not entirely new disciplines. The piece is a useful counterweight to vendor-driven AI safety theater that can lead to over-investment in compliance theater while under-investing in operational reliability. However, the argument understates emerging AI-specific risks like adversarial prompting, model drift, and autonomous agent scope creep that have no direct analogue in prior computing eras.",
        "vendorLean":  "Neutral",
        "imageUrl":  "https://www.infoworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/4229347-0-51631600-1790931753-shutterstock_1743545321.jpg",
