        "url":  "https://www.cio.com/article/4222319/connecting-ai-agents-was-only-the-beginning-now-they-need-to-think-together.html",
        "date":  "2026-09-16",
        "theme":  "Orchestration",
        "summary":  "The article examines the emerging gap between agent connectivity and agent coordination, arguing that linking AI agents across organizational boundaries does not guarantee they will align on goals, context, or meaning. Cisco\u0027s Outshift is developing an \u0027Internet of Cognition\u0027 architecture with shared-intent protocols, cognition fabrics for context management, and negotiation engines to address this. Research cited shows that without these coordination layers, multi-agent systems fail in critical workflows.",
        "execSummary":  "CIOs investing in multi-agent architectures face a second-order problem that connectivity alone does not solve: agents can be technically linked yet fail to share understanding of goals and context, producing misaligned or unaccountable outputs in production workflows. This matters because most enterprise agent deployments are currently at the integration stage, and organizations that don\u0027t architect for cognitive coordination now will hit reliability walls as agent networks grow. Cisco\u0027s Internet of Cognition framing is early-stage but signals where the orchestration market is heading—leaders should evaluate orchestration vendors not just on connectivity standards (MCP, A2A) but on context management and intent-alignment capabilities. The key caveat: this is largely pre-production research; enterprises should pilot carefully in bounded workflows before expanding cross-boundary agent networks.",
