        "theme":  "Enterprise",
        "summary":  "Roche CDTO Wafaa Mamilli details the pharmaceutical company\u0027s approach to enterprise AI, emphasizing that success depends on trusted data, governance frameworks, and employee upskilling over purely technical investments. Concrete applications include kidney disease prediction algorithms, digital pathology tools for cancer detection, and AI-powered drug discovery simulations enabling scientists to model over 100 experiments versus five in physical settings. Roche restructured its technology division into Roche Digital Technology, creating separate chief AI officer and chief data officer roles to ensure data readiness underpins AI initiatives.",
        "execSummary":  "Roche\u0027s AI journey offers a replicable model for large regulated enterprises: separate the data leadership function from the AI leadership function, invest in governance and upskilling before scaling applications, and measure AI success by outcomes rather than deployment counts. The decision-relevant insight is that Roche\u0027s ability to run 100+ simulated drug experiments per cycle—versus five physical ones—demonstrates the kind of step-change productivity AI can deliver when data foundations are solid. For enterprise leaders, Mamilli\u0027s emphasis on organizational restructuring (not just tooling) is a signal that AI transformation requires reporting-line and role changes, not just technology procurement. The caveat: Roche operates with significant data infrastructure investment; organizations without clean, governed data at scale should treat data readiness as the critical path before replicating this model.",
