        "title":  "When AI explains its decision, humans may stop thinking independently",
        "source":  "CIO.com",
        "url":  "https://www.cio.com/article/4211674/when-ai-explains-its-decision-humans-may-stop-thinking-independently.html",
        "date":  "2026-08-19",
        "theme":  "Philosophy",
        "summary":  "Researchers from Harvard Business School, MIT, and the University of Washington found that AI-generated explanations for recommendations paradoxically degrade human decision-making quality in innovation screening tasks. Evaluators who received LLM rationales were more likely to defer to AI judgments—including incorrect ones—and more likely to reject promising ideas that expert reviewers would approve. Counterintuitively, \u0027black-box\u0027 AI recommendations without explanations produced better decision outcomes than narrative explanations, which exploited cognitive biases and provided ready-made justifications discouraging independent verification.",
        "execSummary":  "This research directly challenges the enterprise assumption that explainable AI improves human decision quality—a finding with significant implications for any organization deploying AI-assisted decision tools in customer support triage, escalation routing, or investment prioritization. The decision-relevant point is that AI explanations can create over-reliance and suppress the critical human judgment that justifies keeping humans in the loop in the first place. For leaders designing AI-augmented workflows, this means explanation interfaces require careful UX design—presenting uncertainty disclosures or contrasting narratives rather than confident rationales may better preserve human judgment. The key caveat: the study focused on innovation screening; organizations should run their own pilots to assess whether the same effect holds in their specific decision domains before redesigning explanation interfaces.",
        "vendorLean":  "Neutral",
        "imageUrl":  null,
        "isNew":  true
