        "title":  "CIOs need transparent view of AI deployment",
        "source":  "CIO Dive",
        "url":  "https://www.ciodive.com/news/ai-spending-spreading-beyond-it/831823/",
        "date":  "2026-09-30",
        "theme":  "Strategy",
        "summary":  "BCG research surveying over 1,300 tech leaders found that AI spending has jumped from 1.7% to 3.3% of revenue, with 80% of that spending occurring outside IT budgets. CIOs must evolve into \u0027chief integration officers,\u0027 establishing visibility across distributed AI investments and connecting spending to measurable business value. Success requires focusing 70% of effort on organizational change, processes, and skills development rather than technology alone.",
        "execSummary":  "The most decision-relevant signal here is that 80% of enterprise AI spend is now happening outside IT\u0027s control—in marketing, operations, customer service, and finance—creating an invisible risk portfolio that CIOs are accountable for but cannot see. The BCG data makes a compelling case that the CIO role must transform into an integration and governance function, not a technology delivery function, as AI becomes a distributed business capability. For senior leaders, this reframes the AI investment conversation: the question is no longer \u0027what tools to buy\u0027 but \u0027what governance model allows business units to move fast without creating unmanaged risk.\u0027 The 70/30 split—organizational change over technology—is the key caveat most enterprises are currently inverting.",
